SEEbeck EFFECT
→ 537.6 → Thermoelectricity

A temperature difference becomes electricity.

DISCOVERED
1821  Thomas Johann Seebeck (1770–1831)

PRINCIPLE
Heating one junction of two dissimilar conductors
while keeping the other cooler produces a voltage.
No battery required. The temperature gradient itself
drives charge separation.

FUNDAMENTALS
Conductors: copper and iron (or other dissimilar pair)
Hot junction temperature: T_hot
Cold junction temperature: T_cold
Seebeck coefficient: α  (material-dependent, roughly constant)
Thermoelectric voltage (EMF): V

RELATION
V ≈ α (T_hot − T_cold)

SETUP
Copper wire ── hot liquid (T_hot)
Iron wire   ── ice water (T_cold)
Junctions connected; voltmeter measures generated V

APPLICATIONS
Thermocouples (temperature measurement)
Thermoelectric generators (waste-heat to electricity)

FURTHER WORK
Peltier cooling effects
thermocouple calibration

